  "title": "Fractal KV-Cache Archives: Lossless Symbolic Storage with In-Place Retrieval for Long-Context LLM Inference",
  "event_hint": "arXiv publication on KV cache compression archive format",
  "problem": "KV cache dominates memory cost of long-context autoregressive inference; once quantized to codebook indices, the resulting symbol stream needs storage that supports the access patterns of a growing cache (random access and append)",
  "mechanism": "Contractive iterated-map code (fractal archive) that serializes symbol sequences into low-dimensional real vectors, providing lossless storage with O(1) random access and O(1) amortized append, while simultaneously functioning as a search index for approximate substring queries",
  "evidence": [
    {
      "claim": "Codec encoding runs in linear time at 0.68µs per character",
      "value": "0.68µs",
      "baseline": null,
      "condition": "on natural text, 25K-400K characters",
      "source_locator": "Table 1, Section 2"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Random access costs 311µs per lookup",
      "value": "311µs",
      "baseline": null,
      "condition": "10^6-character document, decoding only the span containing the target",
      "source_locator": "Table 1, Section 2"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Append operation costs 175µs per token (amortized)",
      "value": "175µs",
      "baseline": null,
      "condition": "token-by-token append, re-walking only from last stored anchor",
      "source_locator": "Table 1, Section 2"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Per-head hybrid bit-asymmetric RVQ (K×4, V×2) achieves 36x compression vs fp16 at +11.2% perplexity cost",
      "value": "36x",
      "baseline": "fp16 cache (36,864 B/token)",
      "condition": "GPT-2 124M, 1024-token contexts, 4-sink + 32-recent exact window",
      "source_locator": "Table 2, Section 3"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Quantizing keys is ~4x more damaging than quantizing values",
      "value": "4x",
      "baseline": "value-only quantization (+4.0% perplexity) vs key-only quantization (+14.5% perplexity)",
      "condition": "Two-stage per-head RVQ, GPT-2, 1024-token contexts",
      "source_locator": "Table 2, Section 3 text"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Archive-based search index achieves 1.00 recall for approximate substring queries",
      "value": "1.00",
      "baseline": null,
      "condition": "Nearest-neighbor distance thresholding on stored vectors, query encoded from origin",
      "source_locator": "Figure 3, Section 5"
    }
  ],
  "evaluation_context": "GPT-2 (124M parameters) on The Time Machine (Project Gutenberg public domain text), 1024-token context windows, 4 attention sinks + 32 recent tokens kept exact, remainder archived. Perplexity measured token-by-token, baseline perplexity 35.97. Codebooks trained by k-means on disjoint span of same book. All experiments run on single laptop CPU within minutes.",
  "limitations": "Evaluated only on GPT-2 (124M) - not validated on production-scale models. Archive format itself produces size comparable to bit-packing; compression ratio comes from upstream vector quantizer. Lossy regime (truncated points) forfeits random access and in-place search properties. Multi-span truncation damages recent context as much as distant context, requiring explicit exact-window policy rather than free recency gradient.",
  "project_relevance": "Directly addresses KV cache compression (36x vs fp16) which reduces transfer bytes for cross-region KV cache scenarios. Archive format's O(1) random access enables efficient retrieval patterns in distributed cache settings. Search index capability (1.00 recall) could enable content-based routing/prefetching across regions. Key/value asymmetry insight could inform selective compression strategies for transfer optimization.",
  "primary_source_resolved": true,
  "quality_score": 75,
  "evidence_gaps": [
    {
      "question": "What is the end-to-end bandwidth reduction and transfer time impact for cross-region KV cache deployment?",
      "terms": ["WAN", "bandwidth", "transfer", "network", "latency", "RTT", "distributed"]
    },
    {
      "question": "How does this approach scale to production-scale models (hundreds of kilobytes per token) and what are the consistency/maintenance overheads?",
      "terms": ["production", "scale", "consistency", "maintenance", "overhead", "multi-GPU", "cluster"]
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the incremental encoding/update patterns for streaming contexts and partial cache invalidation?",
      "terms": ["incremental", "streaming", "update", "delta", "invalidation", "append-only"]
    }
  ]
}

